/* ---- the field --------------------------------------------------------
   The products float free. Some of v2's products are rebuilt here as live
   three.js models, and in the hero each one is out in the room on its own
   -- no card, no badge, no shadow -- in front of a stream of light pouring
   out from behind the copy (see makeStream). v2's photo cards, a product
   shot under a Product or Collection badge and a two-line title, are only
   the fallback, for a room without WebGL or with every model switched
   off. (The shelf's 3D cards keep the card: the product standing on its
   photo's own backdrop.)

   Motion is v2's vanishing point, slowed right down once a card is in
   place. To the script everything in the room is a card, a product's card
   being only its box. A card has two depths that start out as one: p, how
   far out it is along its line from the copy's centre, and f, how big it
   is, each a multiple of its home value. In the entrance, both rise from a
   speck behind the headline to home on an eased quartic in ln, and land
   already moving, so there is no stop, no shrink and no overshoot. From
   home on, ln p keeps rising a few per cent a second and ln f at half
   that, so a card slides outward faster than it grows -- a slow swell --
   until it bleeds off the edge of the room. Each slot of the layout is a
   lane. Once the room is out, a lane's next product doesn't fly out: it
   fades in a little short of home, already at the room's slow pace, timed
   to reach home as the last one clears it. The cards stack by depth (the
   bigger on screen, the nearer) and the entrance runs front to back, the
   3D products first, so a card always comes out from behind the ones
   already there. Hovering, holding or having just let go of a product
   holds its whole lane still until you leave it.

   One WebGL context draws every product, and the stream has one of its
   own. Each product has a 2D canvas of its own in its card; per frame the
   shared renderer draws it into a corner of its own canvas and the card
   copies it out. That keeps every product inside its own DOM node, so
   they stack and blur like any other element -- one big overlay canvas
   would have painted every product over every photo card, and couldn't
   blur one alone. A product takes the pointer only over itself, through a
   box that follows it as it lifts (see hitBox): the room round it is the
   page's. */
